Yes, UTMA money can pay for college, since it belongs to the child and can be spent on anything that benefits the child, including tuition. A UTMA has no tax shelter, so selling holdings to cover a tuition bill can create capital gains taxed under the kiddie tax. On the FAFSA, the account counts as a student asset assessed at 20%, compared with up to 5.64% for a parent-owned 529. Once the child reaches majority, control passes to the child, who then decides how the money is spent; the full rules are in [UTMA custodial account explained](/guides/utma-custodial-account-explained/).